    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "The fact that the interview panel was comprised entirely of four men, combined with a significant gap in years of experience and project leadership, is understandable cause for concern. \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of what this may signify, whether to raise it, and practical next steps.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is it a sign of anything?\n\nAn all-male panel is not definitive proof of deliberate discrimination, but it is often indicative of several underlying factors:\n\n* **Affinity Bias (Unconscious Bias):** Human beings have a well-documented tendency to gravitate toward candidates who look, communicate, or socialise like themselves (\"similar-to-me\" bias). When selection criteria hinge on subjective qualities such as *\"excellent client relationships\"* or *\"cultural fit\"*, affinity bias can easily outweigh objective measures like years of design experience, degrees, or project scale.\n* **Firm Demographics and Culture:** An all-male panel for a senior appointment suggests that senior leadership or equity partnership at this consultancy is heavily male-dominated. It also indicates the firm has not adopted modern HR recruitment governance—many consultancies now mandate diverse interview panels precisely to mitigate gender and cognitive bias.\n* **Role Focus (Commercial vs. Technical):** \"Head of Group\" roles often straddle two profiles: technical directorship and commercial/business development. An emphasis on \"client relationships\" suggests the panel may have prioritised immediate commercial networks or rainmaking over technical/delivery depth. That said, subjective rationales are also the easiest to use when justifying a preferred candidate after the fact.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you make something of it?\n\nHow you approach this depends on whether you are considering **formal legal/HR challenge** or using it as a **career signal**.\n\n#### A. If considering formal action or alleging discrimination:\n* **The evidentiary bar is high:** The panel's gender composition alone is generally not enough to sustain a claim of unlawful gender discrimination. While statistical underrepresentation and subjective hiring criteria are relevant factors, employers usually defend these decisions by pointing to interview scoring, commercial fit, or specific client-facing competencies.\n* **Reputational dynamics:** Engineering disciplines and local consultancy markets tend to be close-knit. Raising an allegation of bias without concrete documentation or formal legal advice can risk burning bridges across the local market.\n* **Legal counsel:** If you genuinely believe gender bias was the operative factor in passing you over despite markedly superior qualifications, speak confidentially with an employment lawyer or your professional institution’s legal helpline (e.g., ICE, IStructE, or your local engineering chamber) **before** raising it with the firm.\n\n#### B. As an evaluative signal for your career:\n* It is a very clear signal about the firm’s current leadership environment. If a firm lacks gender balance on senior selection panels and is slow or opaque in providing constructive debriefs, it may be an environment where progression for women continues to face friction.\n* Knowing this can help you decide whether this is an organisation worth pursuing in the future.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up professionally on the feedback (without mentioning bias yet):**\n   Two weeks is an appropriate window to send a polite nudge. Frame the request around professional development and benchmarking:\n   > *\"I am following up on my request for written feedback regarding the Head of Structural Engineering appointment. As a shortlisted candidate with 15 years of design and project leadership experience, specific feedback on where the panel felt my profile differed from the requirements—particularly regarding commercial or client-facing criteria—would be invaluable for my continuous development.\"*\n\n2. **Evaluate the response (or continued silence):**\n   * **If they provide detailed, criteria-based notes:** Review how they scored you against the job description and whether the scoring appears fair, consistent, and objective.\n   * **If the response is vague, dismissive, or ignored:** A failure to provide debriefs to senior shortlist candidates reflects poor HR governance and reinforces the likelihood that subjective criteria drove the outcome.\n\n3. **Leverage your market position:**\n   With a master's degree, chartered status, 15 years of design experience, and proven delivery of major projects, you possess high-demand qualifications. If this consultancy has an insular leadership culture, focusing your leadership ambitions on firms with proven diversity in senior management will likely offer a better platform for your career.\n",
